\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Eudeve (ISO 639-3 eud; also Heve) is an extinct Southern Uto-Aztecan (Taracahitic) language of northern Sonora, Mexico, extinct since about the 1930s. It is closely related to Opata, and some scholars treat the two as dialects. It is known mainly from colonial-era manuscripts. This document therefore supports heritage and revitalization work, not a living-language curriculum.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Eudeve were found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item A Grammatical Sketch of the Heve Language (Buckingham Smith's 1861 translation of an anonymous manuscript), Project Gutenberg: \url{https://gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14419/pg14419-images.html}
\item Andr\'es Lionnet, relaciones internas de la rama sonorense (Amerindia): \url{https://amerindia.cnrs.fr/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/relaciones-internas-de-la-rama-sonorense-Andre%CC%81-LIONNET.pdf}
\item Wikipedia, Eudeve language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eudeve_language}
\item Wikipedia, Opata language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opata_language}
\item Grambank entry: \url{https://grambank.clld.org/languages/eude1234}
\end{itemize}
Lionnet's book \emph{Un idioma extinto de Sonora: El Eudeve} (UNAM, 1986) is mentioned in the results but no online copy was seen.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
ASJP wordlist: \url{https://asjp.clld.org/languages/EUDEVE}. No audio or software found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Heritage material would have to be reconstructed from the colonial grammar and Lionnet's work, and developed with descendant communities in Sonora.

\end{document}
